{"chunk_id": "059078fb2ddf", "text": "Narada finishes describing the celestial assembly of Brahma, a radiant court where gods, sages, and many classes of beings gather and are honored by the creator. He tells Yudhishthira that while human courts may be splendid, Brahma\u2019s is unmatched in all worlds. Curious about the heavenly courts Narada mentioned earlier, Yudhishthira asks why only one mortal king\u2014Harishchandra\u2014dwells in Indra\u2019s assembly. Narada explains that Harishchandra conquered the earth and performed the mighty Rajasuya sacrifice with unmatched generosity, giving wealth far beyond what was asked. Because of this achievement he attained Indra\u2019s realm. Narada then relays a message from Yudhishthira\u2019s deceased father Pandu: Yudhishthira should conquer the earth and perform the Rajasuya so that Pandu too may reach Indra\u2019s heaven. Narada warns the rite is dangerous and may provoke conflict, then departs, leaving Yudhishthira and his brothers contemplating the great sacrifice.\nNarada explains Harishchandra\u2019s greatness and conveys Pandu\u2019s message urging Yudhishthira to perform the Rajasuya sacrifice despite its grave risks.\nThemes: royal sacrifice, kingly duty, fame and merit, ancestral aspiration, cosmic order", "vector": [0.016274644061923027, -0.015868455171585083, 0.0012549551902338862, -0.003303671721369028, 0.00702030211687088, 0.007108309771865606, -0.02540036104619503, -0.019889727234840393, -0.014094763435423374, -0.005706957541406155, -0.00045484720612876117, -0.0011407145066186786, 0.006911985110491514, 0.006360244937241077, -0.01068277470767498, 0.010472909547388554, 0.04208119586110115, -0.006614113226532936, 0.017872322350740433, -0.015475805848836899, -0.001802464248612523, -0.020431313663721085, -0.011474843136966228, 0.02063440904021263, -0.027268830686807632, 0.016328804194927216, 0.04617016389966011, -0.01608509011566639, 0.007182777859270573, -0.0007687975885346532, 0.0008034929051063955, 0.00456624